{
  "response": "**VERDICT: REVISE**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: While the component correctly intends to use an in-DOM `role=\"status\"` live region for announcing asynchronous results, it suffers from critical design and architectural flaws. Most egregiously, the `<label>` is hidden with `display: none` under the mistaken belief that this is accessible, leaving users without a persistent visible label, while reactive state coupling causes premature \"No results found\" announcements to fire while the user is actively typing.\n\n---\n\n### Pre-commitment Predictions\n\nBefore auditing the code, based on the component type (**Dynamic Search Results with Live Region**), the expected high-risk design gaps were:\n1. **Live region announcement timing & racing**: Incomplete status handling during loading, or collisions between loading and completion states.\n2. **Missing persistent visible label**: Over-reliance on placeholder text or improperly hidden label elements.\n3. **Missing search landmark**: Failure to wrap search forms in `<search>` or `role=\"search\"`.\n4. **Silent submission failures**: Empty or invalid searches failing without communicating status to assistive technology.\n5. **Safari VoiceOver list flattening**: Removal of list semantics when `list-style: none` is applied to `<ul>`.\n\n*Audit result*: Predictions 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5 were all confirmed in the source code.\n\n---\n\n### Critical Findings (Blocks Access)\n\n#### 1. `<label>` removed from accessibility tree via `display: none` with missing visible label\n- **Location**: `SearchResults.jsx:29` and CSS `.search-container label`\n- **Code**:\n  ```css\n  .search-container label {\n    display: none; /* Hidden but accessible */\n  }\n  ```\n- **Element / Attribute**: `<label htmlFor=\"search-input\">Search</label>` / CSS `display: none`\n- **User Groups Impacted**: Sighted keyboard users, Speech Recognition (Voice Control / Dragon) users, Cognitive accessibility users, Low vision users.\n- **WCAG / APG Citations**: \n  - **WCAG 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions (Level A)**\n  - **WCAG 1.3.1 Info and Relationships (Level A)**\n  - **WCAG 2.5.3 Label in Name (Level A)**\n  - *Known Anti-Pattern #3: ARIA without visible label*\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**:\n  The developer comment `/* Hidden but accessible */` represents a dangerous misconception. In all browser accessibility trees, `display: none` completely prunes the element from the accessibility tree. Screen readers cannot access it. Furthermore, sighted users are left with zero visible label\u2014only transient `placeholder=\"Search for...\"`, which disappears as soon as text is entered. For users with cognitive disabilities, this causes context loss. For speech-input users who command their device with \"Click Search\", speech software targets the `<button>Search</button>` instead of the input field because the input has no visible text label matching its accessible name.\n- **Remediation**:\n  1. Remove `display: none` from `.search-container label`.\n  2. Keep the `<label>` persistently visible above or adjacent to the input.\n  3. Remove the redundant and conflicting `aria-label=\"Search query\"` on `<input>` so the input's accessible name derives cleanly from its visible `<label>`.\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: search_label_display_none\nfingerprint: b8a72c4e91f03d12\nsource: SearchResults.jsx:29 / CSS:12\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 3.3.2, WCAG 1.3.1, WCAG 2.5.3\nsection_508_fpc_context: WCAG 2.0 A/AA (Section 508 E205.4 / 502.3.1)\nseverity: CRITICAL\nperspective_alarms: screen_reader=HIGH, keyboard=LOW, low_vision=HIGH, cognitive=HIGH\nevidence: CSS:12 `.search-container label { display: none; /* Hidden but accessible */ }`\nreproduction_steps: Inspect accessibility tree in DevTools. The input has no linked label; accessible name falls back to aria-label; sighted users have no persistent label.\nexpected_behavior: Visible, persistent <label> associated via htmlFor to input without display:none.\nactual_behavior: Label is removed from accessibility tree and DOM visibility entirely; placeholder serves as sole visual cue.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n---\n\n### Major Findings (Significantly Degrades Experience)\n\n#### 1. Live region announcement false-triggers during active user typing\n- **Location**: `SearchResults.jsx:42-51, 69-71`\n- **Code**:\n  ```jsx\n  {!isLoading && results.length === 0 && query && 'No results found'}\n  ```\n- **Element / Attribute**: `div[role=\"status\"][aria-live=\"polite\"]`\n- **User Groups Impacted**: Screen reader users (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver).\n- **WCAG / APG Citations**:\n  - **WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (Level A)**\n  - **WCAG 4.1.3 Status Messages (Level AA)**\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**:\n  The live region condition couples directly to the raw, uncommitted `query` state rather than a committed search transaction state. Consider this user journey:\n  1. A user searches for a term that yields no results (`results` is `[]`).\n  2. The screen reader announces \"No results found\".\n  3. The user clears the input box (`query` becomes `\"\"`). The live region text becomes empty.\n  4. The user begins typing their next query. On typing the very first character (e.g., `\"c\"`), `query` becomes truthy, `results.length === 0` is still true, and `isLoading` is false.\n  5. The live region immediately mutates from `\"\"` to `\"No results found\"`, firing an audible announcement to the screen reader while the user is actively typing character 1 of their new query. Similarly, `<p className=\"no-results\">` flashes on screen on every keystroke.\n- **Remediation**:\n  Decouple the live region and results display from the raw input `query`. Introduce a `submittedQuery` or `searchStatus` state (`'idle' | 'loading' | 'success' | 'empty' | 'error'`) that is updated **only** when `handleSearch` executes and completes.\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: live_region_typing_leak\nfingerprint: c19d832e54a67f01\nsource: SearchResults.jsx:49\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 4.1.3\nsection_508_fpc_context: not in scope (WCAG 2.1/2.2 AA)\nseverity: MAJOR\nperspective_alarms: screen_reader=HIGH, cognitive=HIGH\nevidence: SearchResults.jsx:49 `{!isLoading && results.length === 0 && query && 'No results found'}`\nreproduction_steps: Perform a search with 0 results. Clear input. Type a single character. Observe live region DOM node change from empty string to 'No results found'.\nexpected_behavior: Status messages announce only upon search completion, never during active input typing.\nactual_behavior: Live region content changes and triggers announcements while the user types in the input.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n#### 2. Silent failure on empty submission without error identification\n- **Location**: `SearchResults.jsx:11`\n- **Code**:\n  ```jsx\n  const handleSearch = async (e) => {\n    e.preventDefault();\n    if (!query.trim()) return;\n  ```\n- **Element / Attribute**: `<form onSubmit={handleSearch}>`\n- **User Groups Impacted**: Screen reader users, Keyboard-only users, Cognitive accessibility users.\n- **WCAG / APG Citations**:\n  - **WCAG 3.3.1 Error Identification (Level A)**\n  - **WCAG 4.1.3 Status Messages (Level AA)**\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**:\n  When a user submits an empty search field (e.g. by pressing Enter while focused in the input), the handler silently returns. No focus change occurs, no live region announcement fires, and no error message is rendered or associated via `aria-describedby`. Screen reader and keyboard users are left stranded, wondering if the application froze or if the keypress was ignored.\n- **Remediation**:\n  Provide an accessible error state: render an error message (e.g., `<p id=\"search-error\" role=\"alert\">Please enter a search term</p>`), link it to the input via `aria-describedby=\"search-error\"`, and set `aria-invalid=\"true\"` on the input.\n\n#### 3. Name, Role, Value conflation on search input (`aria-label` vs visible text vs placeholder)\n- **Location**: `SearchResults.jsx:30-36`\n- **Code**:\n  ```jsx\n  <label htmlFor=\"search-input\">Search</label>\n  <input\n    id=\"search-input\"\n    type=\"text\"\n    value={query}\n    onChange={(e) => setQuery(e.target.value)}\n    placeholder=\"Search for...\"\n    aria-label=\"Search query\"\n  />\n  ```\n- **Element / Attribute**: `aria-label=\"Search query\"`\n- **User Groups Impacted**: Speech Recognition users, Low vision / screen reader users.\n- **WCAG / APG Citations**:\n  - **WCAG 2.5.3 Label in Name (Level A)**\n  - **WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (Level A)**\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**:\n  Per the W3C Accessible Name Computation (accname), `aria-label` overrides both the `<label>` text and the `placeholder`. The accessible name becomes \"Search query\". However, the visible button says \"Search\", the placeholder says \"Search for...\", and the hidden label says \"Search\". If a speech recognition user says \"Click Search\", the engine targets the submit button. There is no visible cue indicating the string \"Search query\".\n- **Remediation**:\n  Delete `aria-label=\"Search query\"`. Use `<input type=\"search\">` and pair it with a visible `<label htmlFor=\"search-input\">Search</label>`.\n\n---\n\n### Minor Findings (Friction but Workaround Exists)\n\n1. **Safari VoiceOver list semantics lost due to `list-style: none`**:\n   - **Location**: CSS `.results-list`\n   - **Code**: `.results-list { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; }`\n   - **Citation**: WCAG 1.3.1 Info and Relationships.\n   - **Why this matters**: In WebKit (Safari), setting `list-style: none` on a `<ul>` intentionally strips list semantics from the accessibility tree. VoiceOver will not announce \"list, 3 items\".\n   - **Fix**: Add `role=\"list\"` to `<ul className=\"results-list\">` to restore list semantics across WebKit browsers.\n\n2. **Missing native search input type**:\n   - **Location**: `SearchResults.jsx:31`\n   - **Code**: `type=\"text\"`\n   - **Citation**: WCAG 1.3.5 / HTML5 Living Standard.\n   - **Why this matters**: Using `type=\"text\"` misses mobile virtual keyboard optimizations (rendering a \"Search\" submit key on iOS/Android keyboards) and native clear functionality.\n   - **Fix**: Change `type=\"text\"` to `type=\"search\"`.\n\n3. **Submit button missing disabled / busy state during search**:\n   - **Location**: `SearchResults.jsx:37`\n   - **Code**: `<button type=\"submit\">Search</button>`\n   - **Citation**: WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value.\n   - **Why this matters**: During `isLoading === true`, the submit button remains fully interactive and clickable. Keyboard or screen reader users can trigger overlapping API requests.\n   - **Fix**: Add `disabled={isLoading}` or `aria-disabled={isLoading}`, and assign `aria-busy={isLoading}` to the container or form.\n\n4. **Missing visible `:focus` and `:focus-visible` indicators**:\n   - **Location**: CSS `.search-container button`\n   - **Code**: `border: none; background-color: #0066cc;`\n   - **Citation**: WCAG 2.4.7 Focus Visible (Level A) and WCAG 2.4.13 Focus Appearance (Level AAA context).\n   - **Why this matters**: The button resets borders without declaring custom `:focus-visible` outline styles. Depending on the browser, the default outline may blend with `#0066cc` or be suppressed.\n   - **Fix**: Add explicit `:focus-visible` styles with a minimum 2px outline and 3:1 contrast offset (e.g., `outline: 2px solid #000; outline-offset: 2px;`).\n\n---\n\n### Enhancements (Best Practice Not Met)\n\n1. **Missing `<search>` landmark or `role=\"search\"`**:\n   - Wrap the `<form>` in an HTML5 `<search>` element or add `role=\"search\"` to `<form>`. This enables screen reader landmark navigation (`D` key in NVDA/JAWS) so users can immediately locate the search facility. (WCAG 1.3.1).\n2. **Missing `aria-controls` relationship**:\n   - The search button/input does not declare an `aria-controls=\"search-results\"` pointing to the container `id=\"search-results\"`. Adding this relationship establishes programmatic linkage between the trigger and the dynamic target.\n3. **Include query in live region announcement parity**:\n   - While the visual message states `No results found for \"query\"`, the live region only announces generic `No results found`. Announcing `No results found for \"${submittedQuery}\"` guarantees informational parity. (WCAG 4.1.3).\n4. **Rapid double announcement collision risk**:\n   - Announcing `\"Searching...\"` and then `\"Found 3 results\"` within 500ms can cause speech output collisions in VoiceOver and NVDA. Consider dropping the transient `\"Searching...\"` announcement from the live region and relying on `aria-busy=\"true\"` on the results container, letting the live region announce only the terminal completion state.\n\n---\n\n### What's Missing (Gaps, Unhandled Edge Cases)\n\n- **Missing Error Handling**: If an API request encounters a network failure or 500 error, there is no `try/catch` or error state. `isLoading` stays `false` and nothing is displayed or announced.\n- **Missing Landmark Structure**: The component uses generic `<div>` containers. It lacks a `<search>` landmark and an `<h1>` heading on the page before `<h2>Search Results</h2>`.\n- **Missing Results Interactivity**: Search result items render as static text (`<h3>`, `<p>`) rather than links or interactive elements. Keyboard users have nothing to navigate to within the results.\n\n---\n\n### Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n- **Screen reader user**:\n  Experiences severe confusion if searching after a failed query: as soon as they type one character to begin a new search, the live region speaks \"No results found\". List item counts are suppressed in Safari because `list-style: none` removes list semantics. Search landmark is absent.\n- **Keyboard-only user**:\n  Can submit the form via Enter, but receives zero feedback if the field is empty. No custom focus indicator is defined for the submit button. Once results appear, there are no interactive result links to tab into.\n- **Low vision user**:\n  No visible `<label>` exists. Sighted low-vision users zooming to 200% must rely on low-contrast placeholder text that disappears once typing begins. Default focus indicators on the dark blue button may be obscured.\n- **Cognitive accessibility**:\n  Vanishing placeholder text removes persistent context. When an empty search is submitted, the lack of any error message or validation cue gives the impression that the interface is unresponsive.\n- **Vestibular & motion**:\n  No animations, parallax, or continuous transitions are present.\n- **Auditory access**:\n  No audio alerts or media elements are used.\n- **Environmental contrast**:\n  Text colors `#0066cc` on white (5.55:1) and `#333333` (12.6:1) pass WCAG AA. However, relying on default browser focus outlines without custom offset risks low contrast against `#0066cc`.\n\n---\n\n### Perspective Alarm Levels\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | **HIGH** | Reactive live region false-announces while typing; label stripped via `display: none`; list semantics lost in Safari |\n| Keyboard-only | **MEDIUM** | Unhandled silent empty submission; unstyled focus indicators on submit button |\n| Low vision | **HIGH** | Absence of visible label; reliance on disappearing placeholder text |\n| Cognitive | **HIGH** | Disappearing label/placeholder; zero feedback on invalid/empty submissions |\n| Vestibular & motion | **LOW** | No motion or animation used |\n| Auditory access | **LOW** | No audio cues or media elements |\n| Environmental contrast | **MEDIUM** | Focus indicator contrast unmanaged on button |\n\n---\n\n### Realist Check & Severity Calibration\n\n1. **Label `display: none`**: Rated **CRITICAL**. Sighted users have no visible label, speech recognition users cannot target the field by visible name, and screen readers lose the `<label>` node entirely. It violates three distinct Level A criteria (3.3.2, 1.3.1, 2.5.3).\n2. **Typing-triggered live region announcement**: Rated **MAJOR**. While it does not block navigation, hearing \"No results found\" as soon as you type the first letter of a new search is disorienting and violates status message predictability.\n3. **Empty submit silent return**: Rated **MAJOR**. It fails basic error feedback requirements, but keyboard users can recover by typing characters.\n\n---\n\n### Remediation Code\n\n```jsx\nimport React, { useState } from 'react';\n\nconst SearchResults = () => {\n  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');\n  const [submittedQuery, setSubmittedQuery] = useState('');\n  const [results, setResults] = useState([]);\n  const [status, setStatus] = useState('idle'); // 'idle' | 'loading' | 'success' | 'empty' | 'error'\n  const [errorMessage, setErrorMessage] = useState('');\n\n  const handleSearch = async (e) => {\n    e.preventDefault();\n    const trimmed = query.trim();\n\n    if (!trimmed) {\n      setErrorMessage('Please enter a search term.');\n      setStatus('error');\n      return;\n    }\n\n    setErrorMessage('');\n    setSubmittedQuery(trimmed);\n    setStatus('loading');\n    setResults([]);\n\n    try {\n      // Simulate API call\n      setTimeout(() => {\n        const mockResults = [\n          { id: 1, title: 'Result 1', description: 'Description for result 1' },\n          { id: 2, title: 'Result 2', description: 'Description for result 2' },\n          { id: 3, title: 'Result 3', description: 'Description for result 3' },\n        ];\n        setResults(mockResults);\n        setStatus(mockResults.length > 0 ? 'success' : 'empty');\n      }, 500);\n    } catch (err) {\n      setErrorMessage('Search service unavailable. Please try again.');\n      setStatus('error');\n    }\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <search className=\"search-container\">\n      <form onSubmit={handleSearch} noValidate>\n        <label htmlFor=\"search-input\" className=\"search-label\">\n          Search\n        </label>\n        <div className=\"search-input-group\">\n          <input\n            id=\"search-input\"\n            type=\"search\"\n            value={query}\n            onChange={(e) => {\n              setQuery(e.target.value);\n              if (errorMessage) setErrorMessage('');\n            }}\n            placeholder=\"Search for...\"\n            aria-describedby={errorMessage ? 'search-error' : undefined}\n            aria-invalid={status === 'error'}\n          />\n          <button type=\"submit\" disabled={status === 'loading'}>\n            {status === 'loading' ? 'Searching...' : 'Search'}\n          </button>\n        </div>\n\n        {errorMessage && (\n          <p id=\"search-error\" className=\"error-message\" role=\"alert\">\n            {errorMessage}\n          </p>\n        )}\n      </form>\n\n      {/* Persistent live region announces status upon completion */}\n      <div\n        role=\"status\"\n        aria-live=\"polite\"\n        aria-atomic=\"true\"\n        className=\"sr-only\"\n      >\n        {status === 'loading' && 'Searching...'}\n        {status === 'success' && `Found ${results.length} results for ${submittedQuery}`}\n        {status === 'empty' && `No results found for ${submittedQuery}`}\n      </div>\n\n      <div\n        className=\"results-container\"\n        aria-busy={status === 'loading'}\n        id=\"search-results\"\n      >\n        {status === 'success' && (\n          <>\n            <h2>Search Results</h2>\n            <ul className=\"results-list\" role=\"list\">\n              {results.map((result) => (\n                <li key={result.id}>\n                  <h3>\n                    <a href={`#result-${result.id}`}>{result.title}</a>\n                  </h3>\n                  <p>{result.description}</p>\n                </li>\n              ))}\n            </ul>\n          </>\n        )}\n\n        {status === 'empty' && (\n          <p className=\"no-results\">No results found for \"{submittedQuery}\"</p>\n        )}\n      </div>\n    </search>\n  );\n};\n\nexport default SearchResults;\n```\n\n```css\n/* Accessible CSS updates */\n.search-container label.search-label {\n  display: block; /* Visible and accessible */\n  margin-bottom: 6px;\n  font-weight: 600;\n  color: #222;\n}\n\n.search-input-group {\n  display: flex;\n  gap: 8px;\n}\n\n.search-container input:focus-visible,\n.search-container button:focus-visible {\n  outline: 2px solid #000;\n  outline-offset: 2px;\n}\n\n.error-message {\n  color: #d32f2f;\n  font-size: 14px;\n  margin-top: 6px;\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### Verdict Justification\n\nThe verdict is **REVISE**. The component attempts good accessibility hygiene (using an existing DOM node with `role=\"status\"` and semantic headings), but fails on foundational design decisions: stripping the visible label via `display: none`, violating Accessible Name in Name guidelines, dropping empty submissions silently, and prematurely firing live announcements while the user types. Upgrading to **ACCEPT** requires decoupling live region state from the raw input string, restoring a persistent visible label, and adding accessible error handling.\n\n---\n\n### Open Questions (Unscored)\n\n1. Should the search results link to distinct URL routes or open details panels? (If so, focus management or anchor navigation will need explicit specification).\n2. Will this search component support autocomplete/typeahead suggestions in the future? (If so, it should be refactored to follow the WAI-ARIA APG Combobox pattern rather than a standalone form submit).\n",
